WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.860
- [Ray] Principles for Success:

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:04.110
An Ultra Mini-Series Adventure

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:07.520
in 30 minutes and in eight episodes.

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:11.163
Episode Five: Everything is a Machine.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.790
Sometimes things happen
that are hard to understand.

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:18.910
Life often feels so
difficult and complicated.

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:21.880
It's too much to take in all at once.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:25.970
My deep pain led me to reflect
deeply on my circumstances.

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:28.120
It also led me to reflect on nature,

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:31.230
because it provides a
guide for what's true.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:34.900
So I thought a lot about how
things work, which helped

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:39.660
to put me and my own
circumstances in perspective.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:43.810
I saw that at the Big Bang,
all the laws and forces

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:46.600
of the universe were created
and propelled forward,

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:50.130
interacting with each other
as a perpetual motion machine

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:54.260
in which all the bits and
pieces coalesce into machines

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:56.760
that work for awhile, fall apart,

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.170
and then coalesce into new machines.

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:02.000
This goes on into eternity.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.240
I saw that everything is a machine.

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:06.740
The structure and evolution of galaxies,

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:09.170
the formation of our own solar system,

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:13.270
the makeup of Earth's
geography and ecosystems,

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:16.070
our economies and markets, and each of us,

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:20.750
we individually are machines
made up of different machines,

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:23.960
our circulatory system, our nervous system

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.640
that produce our thoughts,
our dreams, our emotions,

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:31.180
and all the other aspects
of our distinct characters.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:34.480
All of these different machines
evolve together through time

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:37.750
to produce the realities
we encounter every day.

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:41.110
And I realized that I
was just one tiny bit

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:44.253
in one nanosecond
deciding what I should do.

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:48.320
While that perspective might
sound very philosophical,

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:50.670
I found that it was very practical

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:52.740
because it showed me how I could deal

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:55.410
with my own realities in a better way.

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:58.750
For example, I observed
that most everything happens

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:02.000
over and over again in
slightly different ways,

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.050
some in obvious short term
cycles that are easy to recognize

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:09.583
so we know how to deal with
them, like the 24 hour day,

00:02:09.583 --> 00:02:12.780
some so infrequently that
they haven't occurred

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:15.610
in our lifetimes, and
we're shocked when they do,

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:17.510
like the once in a hundred year storm,

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:21.960
and some we know exist
but are encountering

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:25.820
for the first time, like the
birth of our first child.

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:28.650
Most people mistakenly
treat these situations

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.620
as being unique and deal
with them without having

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:34.120
proper perspective or principles

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:36.030
to help them get through them.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:38.590
I found that if instead of
dealing with these events

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:43.590
as one offs, I could see each
as just another one of those

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:45.670
and approach them in the same way

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:47.680
a biologist might approach an animal,

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:51.580
first identifying its species,
then drawing on principles

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:53.500
for dealing with it appropriately.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:55.890
Because I could see
these events transpire in

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:58.660
pretty much the same ways over and over,

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:02.180
I could more clearly see the
cause-effect relationships

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:05.320
that govern their behaviors,
which allowed me to develop

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:07.430
better principles that I could express

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:09.900
in both words and algorithms.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:13.140
I learned that while most
everyone expects the future

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:16.090
to be a slightly modified
version of the present,

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:18.010
it is typically very different.

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:20.620
That's because people are
biased by recent history

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:23.930
and overlook events that
haven't happened in a long time,

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:26.340
perhaps not even in their lifetime,

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:28.680
but they will happen again.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:31.930
With that perspective, I
realize that what I missed

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:34.460
when I mistakenly called
for a Great Depression

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:36.980
was hidden in the patterns of history

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:40.250
and I could use my newfound
knowledge of these patterns

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:42.380
to make better decisions in the future.

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:44.600
And when I thought about my challenge

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:47.410
balancing risk and reward, I realized

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:50.660
that risk and reward
naturally go together.

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:53.090
I could see that to get
the most out of life,

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:56.490
one has to take more
risks and that knowing

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:59.780
how to appropriately
balance risk and reward

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:03.170
is essential to having
the best life possible.

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:05.140
Imagine you are faced with a choice

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:09.110
of having a safe boring life
if you stay where you are

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:12.330
or having a fabulous
one if you take the risk

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:15.750
of successfully crossing
a dangerous jungle.

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:19.120
That is essentially
the choice we all face.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:21.860
For me, the choice was
clear, but that doesn't mean

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:24.590
the path forward was without challenges.

00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:27.220
I still needed to face two big barriers

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:29.240
that we all must face.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:32.640
In episode six, I'll share
some invaluable techniques

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:34.843
I learned about how to best do that.

